<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened Runtime entitlements for the main app bundle.

  ONE entitlement. That is deliberate. @electron/osx-sign's default darwin
  plist — which is what you get by NOT writing this file — additionally grants
  audio-input, camera, bluetooth, USB, printing and location. This app reads
  .docx files and talks to one HTTPS endpoint from the main process. It has no
  business holding a microphone entitlement, and a notarised build that does is
  a build whose entitlements no longer describe it.

  `com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit` is genuinely required: V8 writes executable
  memory, and Hardened Runtime refuses that without it.

  Deliberately ABSENT, and each absence is load-bearing:
    - allow-unsigned-executable-memory: the blunt-instrument version of the
      above. Modern Electron does not need it; granting it disables a real
      mitigation for the whole process.
    - disable-library-validation: would let ANY dylib signed by anyone load
      into this process. Nothing here loads a third-party native module —
      SQLite comes from node:sqlite, inside Electron's own binary.
    - allow-dyld-environment-variables: an injection vector, wanted only by
      profilers.
    - com.apple.security.cs.debugger: lets the app attach to other processes.
    - App Sandbox entitlements: this is a Developer ID build, not Mac App
      Store. The sandbox is not applied, so listing them would be decoration.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
